From 35a5ce9feef03f5043b9957a698723cee850f470 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:20:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat: a clone numbers its objects into the process record
 its file gives them

Every object a TRef may point at names, above its number, the process that
numbered it, and TRef::Streamer writes that process beside the number - but as
an index into one file's ProcessID records, not into the session's processes.
A clone reads one file and writes another, and the two number their processes
differently: roottest/root/io/tree/test-rcrr123's session adopts the copied
file's process first, so its own - the one TProcessID::AssignID numbers the new
references into - is the second record there and the first here.

- wbranch: tref_record takes the index of the process's record in the file
  being written, which only TFile::WriteProcessID can say.
- trees: a row's identifier and a reference's fPID are rewritten through
  TFile::WriteProcessID as the entry is written, in branch order, which is the
  order ROOT makes its records in; _named_processes hands back the index it
  gave each process so tref_record can name it.
- trees: a clone fills a split collection from the objects its source tree was
  given even when the collection holds a TRef per object. Those objects are the
  ones AssignID numbered as the reference was made, so copying the values last
  read writes the identifiers they had before, without the kIsReferenced bits.

The written file now holds what the oracle's does - N.fUniqueID 0x01000009 in
record 0, the r1 clones' 0x02000009 in record 1, r1's references fPID 0 and
r2's fPID 1, and r2/r3's references resolve to the r1 clones rather than to
whichever collection was filed last.

Still DIFF: reading one back folds a constant 1<<24 into every identifier
(interp._numbered), so a byte of 2 is read as 3 and the objects of a file's
second record are looked for in a record it does not have. That fold is
interp.py's - a _numbered that folded the record's *session* index, as ROOT's
reader does from the pidf it reads after fBits, is the other half.
